          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Pierre\",\"addressee\":\"Hélène\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Je vous aime!\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you!\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Pierre's love for Hélène as a current emotional state. Its conventional role in the courtship scene does not remove its truth-conditional avowal force, and Pierre has just inwardly represented the same state to himself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"да и я люблю ее\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal also substantially affirms and ratifies Pierre's position as Hélène's fiancé. The surrounding scene has publicly established them as engaged, and Pierre deliberately supplies what he understands must be said in that situation. Thus the words do not make a separately specified promise, but they enact his acceptance of the relational undertaking already being imposed and recognized around them.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is not represented as a reflex-like affective discharge. Pierre pauses, recalls the conventional words required by the occasion, and then says them; the exclamation mark does not outweigh that deliberative framing.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях; но слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"No distinct residual core function is needed: the occurrence is adequately characterized as an avowal of love that also affirms the engagement undertaking in this context.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Pierre and Hélène have been treated by their families and guests as an engaged couple without Pierre having clearly proposed. After Prince Vasili publicly blesses them, they are left alone; Hélène kisses Pierre, and Pierre, thinking that everything is already settled, recalls and utters the conventional declaration expected on such an occasion.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The undertaking is Pierre's affirmation and acceptance of the fiancé–fiancée bond, rather than a new promise with separately stated future terms. Hélène has already participated in the intimate and social enactment of the engagement, and the narration calls her his fiancée. There is no direct verbal reply to the focal words, but the subsequent marriage shows that the engagement was carried through. Pierre's dread, social pressure, physical attraction, self-persuasion, and shame make the depth and sincerity of loving affection uncertain without eliminating the utterance's avowal or undertaking force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognize the supplied passage as Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace, depicting Pierre Bezukhov's engagement to Hélène Kuragina.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence gives unusually rich access to Pierre's hesitation and the social pressure surrounding the declaration, but it does not provide Hélène's direct verbal response to the focal utterance. Pierre's thought that he loves her supports affection, while the narration's emphasis on attraction, dread, convention, and embarrassment prevents a confident conclusion about the quality or durability of that affection.\"}"
